.TH HWDP 1 "2026" "hwdp" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hwdp \- hardware display-profile detection, layout, and autoscale suite
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hwdp
recognises which monitors are present and lays out / autoscales the workspace
for them, keyed on the HARDWARE (an EDID-derived display-profile id), never on
a hostname. It installs ONE command,
.BR hwdp ,
whose subcommands are executables under libexec/hwdp/cmd/, so adding one is
adding a file, plus the standalone clients
.B run-scaled
and
.B wallpaper-slicer
described below. It is environment-agnostic: it works under X11 or a wlroots
compositor, and hardcodes no downstream tool \(em everything session-specific
is a HOOK or a PROVIDER an integrator fills.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B hwdp id
The display-profile id for the connected monitor set: a compound hash over the
connected connectors' EDIDs, so it names the SET regardless of order or which
connector each panel sits on. Empty with nothing connected. Resolves HEADLESS.
.TP
.B hwdp shape
The workspace shape \(em
.BR single " or " triple
\(em the panel COUNT coarsened, since the grid, window rules and wallpaper key
on how many panels there are, not which. Resolves HEADLESS.
.TP
.B hwdp ui
Per-display UI sizing as shell-sourceable KEY=VALUE
.RB ( KITTY_FONT ", " TITLE_FONT ", " MAKO_FONT ", " LOCK_RADIUS ", "
.BR CURSOR_SIZE ),
for apps whose size is a single global config and so cannot self-scale per
output. An optional
.I <id>.ui
override wins per key; else a density default. Resolves HEADLESS, and REFUSES
(exit 3, no output) rather than guess when no display answers at all.
.TP
.B hwdp geometry
Print one line per enabled output \(em
.I NAME MODE_W MODE_H TRANSFORM SCALE POS_X POS_Y LOG_W LOG_H ORIENTATION
\(em projected from the shared layout probe. Settles by default (samples until
the layout holds still);
.B \-\-now
prints one snapshot.
.TP
.B hwdp layout
Emit the runtime kanshi config for the connected set and print its path: inject
each output's sticky scale into the profile named by the id, or synthesize a
side-by-side one when there is none.
.TP
.B hwdp capture
Snapshot the current arrangement (transforms, positions, modes \(em no scale)
into a profile named by the id, so a rig is onboarded by arranging it once.
.TP
.B hwdp watch
Supervise the layout for a session (supervise-or-nudge via
.BR flock ),
regenerate the config through
.BR "hwdp layout" ,
and re-apply on compositor-config changes. It fires display-change HOOKS from
.IR <root>/<edge>.d/* " (edges: " pre ", " changed )
so an integrator wires the reactions; it knows nothing of any specific
compositor tool.
.PP
It reacts to the DISPLAY SET changing as well as to the compositor config: the
connected panels are polled every
.B HWDP_HOTPLUG_POLL
seconds (default 4, 0 disables), and on a change the kanshi config is
REGENERATED before the hooks run and kanshi reloads \(em its profiles describe
the outputs that existed when it was written, so a reload alone would re-read a
file that does not mention the new panel. The fingerprint is the same probe
.B hwdp id
hashes, so a monitor swapped on one connector counts even though that connector
never leaves `connected'. Hook roots:
.BR HWDP_HOOK_ROOT " (default ~/.config/hwdp/hooks), " HWDP_MACHINE_HOOKS
(default /etc/hwdp/hooks).
.SH MIXED DPI
Panels of different physical density need
.BR HWDP_TARGET_PPI .
.B hwdp ui
emits one value per key because each consumer has one global config \(em kitty
a single font_size, pixdecor a single compositor-wide title_font, mako a single
font \(em so there is nowhere to put a per-output value. The only fix is to
remove the difference: set
.B HWDP_TARGET_PPI=<n>
and
.B hwdp layout
scales each output, from its EDID millimetre size, so every one lands near that
LOGICAL density. One set of numbers is then correct everywhere, and
.B hwdp ui
sizes for the target rather than for any one panel.
.PP
The cost is logical desktop AREA, not sharpness: with
wp_fractional_scale_manager_v1 a 4K at 1.75 still draws text at 1.75x device
pixels; what shrinks is the logical desktop (3840x2160 to 2194x1234).
.PP
Left unset, every output stays at scale 1 and density is absorbed by the UI
numbers instead \(em exact for one panel or a wall of identical ones. Seeing
panels more than 25% apart,
.B hwdp ui
then names them with their ppi on stderr, sizes for the DENSEST (oversized on a
coarse panel is clumsy but readable; the reverse is microscopic), still emits a
full key set and still exits 0.
.PP
A scale below 1 is always clamped to 1.00 and reported: it cannot render, so
writing it would make the config claim a scale the session never has.
.SH CLIENTS
Two further commands ship alongside
.BR hwdp ,
deliberately NOT subcommands of it: every subcommand above reads or manages
display state and returns, where these CONSUME that state to do something else.
Both read
.B "hwdp geometry"
or
.B "hwdp ui"
as ordinary clients, exactly as an integrator's own tool would.
.TP
.B run-scaled
Takes someone else's command line and BECOMES that process, so its exit status
and stdout are the child's. It magnifies a single application via a nested
.B gamescope
window (renders at a size/scale and upscales), windowed or fullscreen, for
fixed-layout programs that cannot scale themselves. The default magnification
comes from
.BR "hwdp ui" 's
.B MAGNIFY
key, so the factor is a property of the DISPLAY rather than something each
caller hardcodes.
.TP
.B wallpaper-slicer
Cuts one image into per-output crops that TILE across a multi-output layout.
Wayland clients that draw a wallpaper (swaylock, swaybg, a greeter background)
paint the same image on every output and have no spanned mode, so the source is
pre-sliced: each crop is that output's sub-rectangle of the image after it is
cover-scaled (fill) or contained (fit) over the whole layout union. Every slice
comes from one global cover rect, so a feature crossing a bezel stays
continuous. Slices are cut in LOGICAL orientation and never pre-rotated \(em
the compositor applies the output transform. Prints
.IR NAME <TAB> PATH
per output; needs one of
.BR magick ", " convert " or " vips .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/hwdp/hooks/<edge>.d/*
User display-change hooks run by `hwdp watch'.
.TP
.I ~/.config/kanshi/{profiles,auto-scale}
Layout templates and the sticky per-panel scale overrides.
.TP
.I ~/.config/hwdp/providers/{layout,panels}/*
User display probe providers, tried before the machine and shipped ones.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B HWDP_HOOK_ROOT ", " HWDP_MACHINE_HOOKS
Roots for `hwdp watch's display-change hooks.
.TP
.B HWDP_PROVIDER_ROOT ", " HWDP_MACHINE_PROVIDERS
Roots for display probe providers. Each holds a
.I layout
and/or
.I panels
directory whose executables are tried in filename order; the first to exit 0
with output wins, and one that cannot answer in this environment exits
non-zero and is skipped.
.TP
.B HWDP_GEOM_BACKEND
An executable tried as the first layout provider, ahead of every directory.
.TP
.B HWDP_DRM
The DRM sysfs root the shipped panels provider reads (default
.IR /sys/class/drm ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR kanshi (1),
.BR wlr-randr (1),
.BR gamescope (1)
